Two Sonnets That Say Hi and Ho.

Romeo, a young man who answers in kind.
Juliet, a young woman who speaks in sonnets.

                    Act I: An exchange of sonnets.

                    Scene I: Juliet greets the world, and Romeo replies.

[Enter Romeo and Juliet]

Juliet:
 Thou art twice twice twice the sum of an elf
 and a big big big cat. Speak thy mind! And
 thou art the sum of the sum of thyself
 and a fig and a big big big big big hand.
 Speak thy mind! Thou art the sum of a cat
 and a big big big big big dog. Speak thy
 mind! Thou art the sum of a big big big bat
 and a big fly. Speak thy mind! Thou art a sky.
 Thou art a hat. Thou art a log. Thou art
 a hog. Remember thee. Recall thy dog.
 Thou art the sum of a cat and a cart.
 Thou art a big big frog. Thou art a log.
 Thou art the sum of a rat and a bee.
 And thou art the sum of thyself and me.

Romeo:
 Thou art twice twice twice the sum of a cow
 and a big big big hat. Speak thy mind! Thou art
 the sum of thee and the sum of an old bough
 and the sum of a big big big big big cart
 and the sum of a big big fox and a fly.
 Speak thy mind! Thou art the sum of a dog
 and a big big big big big fly. Speak thy
 mind! Thou art the sum of a big big big frog
 and a big log. Speak thy mind! Thou art a bee.
 Thou art a hat. Remember thee. Thou art a cat.
 Thou art the sum of a rat and a tree.
 Thou art a log. Thou art a big big rat.
 Thou art the sum of a fox and a fig.
 And thou art the sum of thyself and a pig.

[Exeunt]
